Wind turbines break down unexpectedly, causing costly downtime and rushed repairs. 
This project builds a machine learning system that reads turbine sensor data — temperature, vibration, rotor speed, and more — and predicts failures before they happen. 
It also identifies the specific fault type (Gearbox, Blades, or Generator) so maintenance teams know exactly what to fix.
The pipeline covers the full data science workflow: data cleaning, exploratory analysis, feature engineering, model training, and evaluation. 
Five models were compared — Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and XGBoost. 
SMOTE was applied to the training set to handle class imbalance, since failures make up only 12.8% of the data.

XGBoost delivered the strongest results:

AUC-ROC: 1.00
Recall (Failure class): 1.00
F1-Score (Failure class): 0.99
Precision (Failure class): 0.99
5-Fold Cross-Validation AUC: 1.00 ± 0.00
Fault Type Classification Accuracy: 1.00 across all three categories (Gearbox, Blades, Generator)

SHAP explainability was applied to make every prediction transparent.
Instead of a black-box alert, the system shows which sensor readings drove the prediction — making it practical for engineers in the field, not just data scientists.
